******************************************************* Hibernate.cfg.xml ********************************************************
<hibernate-configuration>
    <!-- Session Factory Configuration -->
    <session-factory>
        
        <!-- Dialect: Specifies the database dialect for Hibernate to use (e.g., MySQL, PostgreSQL) -->
        <property name="hibernate.dialect">org.hibernate.dialect.MySQLDialect</property>

        <!-- JDBC URL: The database URL where your DB is located -->
        <property name="hibernate.connection.url">jdbc:mysql://localhost:3306/alien</property>
        
        <!-- Username for connecting to the database -->
        <property name="hibernate.connection.username">root</property>
        
        <!-- Password for connecting to the database -->
        <property name="hibernate.connection.password">password</property>

        <!-- Driver class name: MySQL JDBC driver -->
        <property name="hibernate.connection.driver_class">com.mysql.cj.jdbc.Driver</property>

        <!-- Hibernate's automatic schema generation (create, update, validate, etc.) -->
        <property name="hibernate.hbm2ddl.auto">update</property>

        <!-- Hibernate shows SQL statements in the console for debugging -->
        <property name="hibernate.show_sql">true</property>

        <!-- Formatting SQL output -->
        <property name="hibernate.format_sql">true</property>

        <!-- Caching configuration: Disables 2nd level cache for simplicity -->
        <property name="hibernate.cache.provider_class">org.hibernate.cache.internal.NoCacheProvider</property>

        <!-- Set the Hibernate’s session context to ‘thread’ (default) or ‘jta’ for JTA transactions -->
        <property name="hibernate.transaction.coordinator_class">org.hibernate.resource.transaction.backend.jdbc.internal.JdbcTransactionFactory</property>
        
        <!-- Enable Hibernate's second-level cache (optional for production) -->
        <property name="hibernate.cache.use_second_level_cache">false</property>

    </session-factory>
</hibernate-configuration>


Explaination:
1. The hibernate.cfg.xml file is where you configure Hibernate's settings and provide details about your database connection. It includes database credentials, Hibernate-specific settings, and mappings for your entities.

2. Explanation of each line:
	<hibernate-configuration>: This is the root tag for Hibernate configuration.

	<session-factory>: All Hibernate configuration settings go inside this tag. It defines the session factory, which is responsible for creating and managing sessions.

	hibernate.dialect: This property specifies the database dialect Hibernate should use. It is database-specific and tells Hibernate how to communicate with your database. For 				   example, MySQLDialect for MySQL.

	hibernate.connection.url: Specifies the JDBC URL for the database connection.

	hibernate.connection.username and hibernate.connection.password: These properties hold the credentials to connect to your database.

	hibernate.connection.driver_class: Specifies the JDBC driver class to use (e.g., for MySQL, com.mysql.cj.jdbc.Driver).

	hibernate.hbm2ddl.auto: This property determines how Hibernate handles the schema (database structure). update will automatically update the database schema based on the entity 				classes.

	hibernate.show_sql: When set to true, this will log the SQL statements executed by Hibernate.

	hibernate.format_sql: This makes the SQL output more readable by formatting it.
	
	hibernate.cache.provider_class: Sets the cache provider class (disabled in this case).
	
	hibernate.transaction.coordinator_class: Defines the transaction management strategy.
	
	hibernate.cache.use_second_level_cache: Disables the second-level cache to simplify this example.

	<mapping class="com.abd.DemoHibernate.Alien"/>: Tag in hibernate.cfg.xml specifies the Java class Alien that Hibernate should map to a database table for ORM operations.

